adjudication.rationale:
In packages/core/EventManager.ts the diff changes destinationCalendar to an array and adds:

```ts
const [mainHostDestinationCalendar] = evt.destinationCalendar ?? [];
if (evt.location === MeetLocationType && mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration !== "google_calendar") {
  ...
}
```

If `evt.destinationCalendar` is `undefined`, `null`, or an empty array, destructuring yields `mainHostDestinationCalendar === undefined`, and accessing `.integration` will throw a TypeError at runtime. This can occur specifically on Google Meet events with no destination calendar, i.e., the fallback path the branch is meant to handle.

In packages/core/EventManager.ts the diff introduces:
```ts
const [mainHostDestinationCalendar] = evt.destinationCalendar ?? [];
if (evt.location === MeetLocationType && mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration !== "google_calendar") {
  ...
}
```
When `evt.destinationCalendar` is `null`, `undefined`, or an empty array, destructuring yields `mainHostDestinationCalendar === undefined`. If `evt.location === MeetLocationType` (Google Meet), the condition evaluates the RHS and dereferences `mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration`, which will throw a runtime TypeError. Previously the code used optional chaining (`evt.destinationCalendar?.integration`) and would not throw in this case.

In packages/core/EventManager.ts the diff changes destinationCalendar to an array and adds:

```ts
const [mainHostDestinationCalendar] = evt.destinationCalendar ?? [];
if (evt.location === MeetLocationType && mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration !== "google_calendar") {
  ...
}
```

If `evt.destinationCalendar` is `null`, `undefined`, or an empty array (which the diff explicitly introduces in multiple places, e.g. `destinationCalendar: selectedDestinationCalendar ? [selectedDestinationCalendar] : []`), then `mainHostDestinationCalendar` will be `undefined` and `mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration` will throw a TypeError at runtime. This is a concrete correctness bug introduced by the diff. The claim about missing test coverage is plausible but not verifiable from the provided diff alone; however the dereference issue itself is real.
